1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,512
Renewable Energy: Solar and Wind Power.

2
00:00:03,462 --> 00:00:09,825
Solar photovoltaic and wind power have transformed the global energy landscape over the past two decades.

3
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:21,450
According to the International Energy Agency, renewables accounted for over 30% of global electricity generation in 2023, with solar and wind comprising the fastest-growing segment.

4
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:36,512
The IEA's Renewables 2023 report projected that global renewable capacity would increase by nearly 2,400 gigawatts between 2022 and 2027 — an expansion roughly equivalent to the entire power capacity of China.

5
00:00:36,512 --> 00:00:39,350
The cost trajectory has been dramatic.

6
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:54,650
The levelised cost of electricity from utility-scale solar PV fell by 89% between 2009 and 2022, from approximately $359 per megawatt-hour to $40 per megawatt-hour, according to IRENA.

7
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:59,475
Onshore wind costs declined by 69% over the same period.

8
00:00:59,475 --> 00:01:08,637
In many regions, including parts of Europe, the Middle East, and Australia, solar and wind are now the cheapest source of new electricity generation.

9
00:01:08,637 --> 00:01:11,625
Technology has driven these gains.

10
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:21,762
Modern wind turbines have grown substantially; the average rotor diameter of newly installed onshore turbines in Europe reached 130 metres in 2022.

11
00:01:21,762 --> 00:01:26,300
Offshore turbines of 15 megawatts each are now commercially available.

12
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:41,750
Solar PV efficiency — the fraction of sunlight converted to electricity — has increased from roughly 15% a decade ago to over 22% in 2023, with perovskite-silicon tandem cells achieving over 33% in laboratory settings.

13
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:50,800
Energy storage costs, primarily lithium-ion batteries, have fallen by over 80% since 2010, addressing the intermittency challenge.

